---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
    { title: "How to Seal a Driveway", url: "/how-to-seal-a-driveway/" },
    { title: "Cleaning Asphalt Before Sealing", url: "/how-to-clean-asphalt-driveway-before-sealing/" },
    { title: "How Many Coats of Sealer?", url: "/how-many-coats-of-driveway-sealer/" },
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-06", modified: "2026-09-06" });
const title = "Why Is My Driveway Sealer Peeling or Flaking?";
const description =
  "Peeling means the sealer never bonded. The six causes, how to tell them apart from what the flakes look like, and what has to be stripped.";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Sealer Peeling or Flaking",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/driveway-sealer-peeling/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why is my driveway sealer peeling off?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Because it never bonded. Something sat between the sealer and the asphalt, most often oil, dust or chalky oxidised surface, or the driveway was damp when it went down. Look at the underside of a flake: clean and glossy means an adhesion failure rather than a product fault.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you seal over peeling sealer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. The new coat bonds to the failing layer rather than the driveway, so it lifts along with it, usually sooner. Everything loose has to be scraped, brushed and washed off first, and the removal should extend past the visibly peeling area.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you seal a driveway too many times?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, and it is a common cause of flaking. Sealer accumulates, and thick buildup goes rigid, loses the flexibility to move with the asphalt, then crazes and sheets off. Flakes with old sealer rather than asphalt attached to the back are the tell.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why is my sealer peeling only in the wheel tracks?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Traffic before the coating fully cured. Sealer is dry to the touch long before it has hardened, and turning tyres apply a shearing force that lifts it. The rest of the driveway being intact is the diagnostic, and the fix is keeping traffic off for the full cure time.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I know if it is the sealer or the driveway failing?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Look at what is stuck to the underside of a flake. Clean and glossy means the sealer let go of the asphalt, which is an adhesion problem you can correct. Asphalt aggregate attached means the surface itself is coming apart, which is a resurfacing question instead.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does oil cause driveway sealer to peel?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, reliably, and at exactly the spots where it was. Oil soaks into porous asphalt and washing does not lift what has penetrated, so it needs a degreaser or an absorbent poultice. Every oil spot left untreated becomes a place the sealer lifts first.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Will pressure washing remove peeling sealer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It removes what is already loose, which is a necessary first step, but it will not strip a fully bonded old coating. Where buildup is the cause and the whole layer has to come off, chemical stripping or mechanical removal is usually the realistic route.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long should I wait before driving on new sealer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Longer than it takes to feel dry, and the product sheet is the authority. Dry, cured and ready for traffic are three different states, and driving too early is one of the most common causes of peeling confined to the wheel tracks.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Driveway & Concrete",
    tags: [
      "driveway sealer peeling",
      "driveway sealer flaking",
      "sealer not adhering",
      "driveway & concrete",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Driveway Sealers",
    link: "/best-driveway-sealer/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Troubleshooting
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Field Tested
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          Why Is My Driveway <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Sealer Peeling?</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Peeling is an adhesion failure, and adhesion is decided before the sealer goes down. That means the cause is almost always in the preparation rather than in the product.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#read-the-flakes"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            Diagnose the Cause
          </a>
          <a
            href="#faq"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Jump to the FAQ
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="11.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#read-the-flakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Read the Flakes</a>
          <a href="#contamination" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Contamination</a>
          <a href="#too-many-coats" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Too Much Sealer</a>
          <a href="#moisture-and-weather" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Moisture and Weather</a>
          <a href="#other-causes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Other Causes</a>
          <a href="#fixing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. How to Fix It</a>
          <a href="#prevention" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Prevention</a>
          <a href="#concrete-vs-asphalt" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Concrete vs Asphalt</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A sealer that peels never stuck in the first place. Something sat between it and the driveway, or the surface underneath was not sound, and the coating has simply been waiting for weather to prove it.
        </p>

        <div id="read-the-flakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Read the Flakes First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before guessing at a cause, look at what is on the underside of a lifted piece. It narrows the diagnosis faster than anything else.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What the Flake Underside Shows</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Most Likely Cause</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clean, glossy, no asphalt attached</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Never bonded: dirt, oil or an old sealer beneath</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fine dust or powder clinging</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Chalky, oxidised surface not cleaned off</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Old sealer attached, not asphalt</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Too many layers; the old coat let go</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Asphalt aggregate attached</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The surface itself is failing, not the sealer</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Soft or gummy underside</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Applied too thick, or over uncured filler</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Peeling only in wheel tracks</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Traffic before full cure</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Peeling only in one area</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Localised contamination, usually oil</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Distinction That Matters</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Sealer failing is fixable. Asphalt failing is not a sealing problem.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the underside of the flake is clean, the sealer let go of the driveway and you have an adhesion problem to correct. If asphalt aggregate is stuck to it, the surface itself came away, which means the driveway is deteriorating underneath and no amount of resealing addresses that. The second case is a resurfacing conversation rather than a sealing one.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="contamination" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Cause One: Something Was in the Way
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common cause by a wide margin. Sealer bonds to asphalt, and it cannot bond to anything sitting on top of the asphalt.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Oil and transmission fluid, which soak in and defeat adhesion completely at that spot.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Dust and fine dirt, which look like nothing and act as a release layer.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Chalky oxidised asphalt, where the surface itself has degraded into loose powder.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Moss, algae and organic growth, especially in shaded areas.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>De-icing salt residue left from the previous winter.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Tyre marks and rubber deposits in turning areas.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Oil is the one to take seriously, because washing does not fix it. It penetrates porous asphalt and no amount of pressure lifts what has gone below the surface, so it needs a degreaser or an absorbent poultice that draws it back out. Every remaining oil spot becomes a place the sealer lifts first. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-oil-stains-from-driveway/">removing oil stains</a> covers the products that work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Chalking is the subtler one. An older driveway that has oxidised sheds a fine powder when you rub it, and sealer applied over that bonds to the powder rather than the asphalt. The test is simple: run a hand across the surface and look at your palm. If it comes away grey, the driveway needs a thorough wash and full drying before anything goes on it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="too-many-coats" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Cause Two: Too Much Sealer
          </h2>

          <p>
            The counterintuitive one, and the reason well-intentioned owners who seal every year end up with the worst-looking driveways on the street.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sealer builds up. Each coat sits on the previous one, and coatings that thick become rigid and brittle, losing the flexibility to move with asphalt as it expands and contracts. Eventually the accumulated layer cracks in a fine spiderweb pattern and begins to flake off in sheets, usually taking the coats below it along.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Look for flakes with old sealer on the underside rather than asphalt, which is the signature of this failure.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Look for a fine crazed pattern across large areas rather than isolated lifting.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Consider how often the driveway has been sealed. Every year for several years is enough to cause it.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Check whether coats were applied too thick, which reaches the same place faster.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Note that thick application also traps moisture and solvent, which weakens every layer above.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            The correct interval is longer than most people assume, and driven by condition rather than the calendar. Our guide to <a href="/how-often-should-you-seal-your-driveway/">how often to seal a driveway</a> covers how to judge it, including the signs you are already sealing too often. Two thin coats applied properly outperform one thick one every time, which our guide to <a href="/how-many-coats-of-driveway-sealer/">how many coats</a> explains.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="moisture-and-weather" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Cause Three: Moisture and Weather
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealer needs the surface dry when it goes down and needs time to cure before anything wets it. Failures here are dramatic and usually appear within days rather than seasons.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Condition</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Does</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Damp surface at application</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Prevents bonding; peels in sheets</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rain within the cure window</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Washes off or leaves a weak, patchy film</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sealing below the minimum temperature</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Never coalesces properly; stays weak</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cold nights after a warm day</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cure stalls even if the afternoon was fine</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dew forming overnight on fresh sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Same effect as light rain</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Applied in direct hot sun</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Skins over fast, traps solvent, lifts later</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water trapped in cracks beneath</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Pushes the coating off locally</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The overnight dew case catches people out most often in autumn. The afternoon is warm and dry, the sealer looks perfect at dusk, and a cold clear night deposits moisture on a coating that had not finished curing. Check the overnight low and the dew point, not just the daytime forecast.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our guides to the <a href="/best-time-to-seal-driveway/">best time to seal a driveway</a> and <a href="/rain-after-driveway-sealing/">rain after driveway sealing</a> cover the temperature, humidity and timing windows, and what to do when the forecast changes mid-job.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="other-causes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Remaining Causes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three more, less common but each responsible for a recognisable pattern of failure.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Traffic Before Cure</h3>

          <p>
            Peeling confined to wheel tracks, with the rest of the driveway intact, is almost always this. Sealer is dry to the touch long before it is cured, and turning tyres apply a shearing force that lifts a coating that has not fully hardened. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-for-driveway-sealer-to-dry/">sealer dry times</a> covers the difference between dry, cured and ready for traffic.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sealing Over Uncured Crack Filler</h3>

          <p>
            Localised soft, wrinkled or lifting patches directly over repairs. The filler was still off-gassing when it was covered, and the trapped solvent kept the sealer above it soft. Most fillers want 24 to 48 hours, and cold patch commonly wants 30 days.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Incompatible Products</h3>

          <p>
            Layering different sealer chemistries can produce poor adhesion, particularly applying certain products over an old coat of a different type. Where the driveway history is unknown, a small test patch left for a few weeks is cheap insurance against redoing the whole surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            That test patch habit is worth adopting generally. A square metre sealed and left through a rain cycle and a warm day tells you more about how the product will behave on your specific driveway than any label claim, and it costs an hour.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fixing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How to Fix It
          </h2>

          <p>
            The unwelcome answer is that peeling sealer has to come off. Coating over a failing layer produces a new coating that fails with it, usually faster.
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Remove everything loose. Scrape, wire brush, and pressure wash until nothing more lifts.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Keep going past the visibly peeling area, since the edge of a failure is rarely where it stops.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Where buildup is the cause, the old sealer generally has to come off entirely rather than partially.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Address the underlying cause: degrease the oil, wash off the chalk, or fix the drainage.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Let the surface dry completely, which after washing means longer than it looks.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Test a small area with the new product and give it a couple of weeks before committing.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span>Apply thin coats, in the right weather, and keep traffic off for the full cure.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            For widespread buildup, chemical stripping or mechanical removal may be the realistic route, and it is genuinely laborious. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-old-driveway-sealer/">removing old driveway sealer</a> covers the methods and what each is worth.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Before You Redo Anything</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Establish whether it was the sealer or the driveway.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the flakes had asphalt aggregate stuck to them, the surface itself is coming apart, and a perfect sealing job over a failing driveway fails too. That is a resurfacing question rather than a sealing one, and it is worth answering before spending another weekend and another set of pails on the same square metres.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="prevention" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Getting It Right Next Time
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every cause above maps to a preparation step, which is where the whole job is won or lost.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Step</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Prevents</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Degrease every oil spot properly</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Localised peeling at the spots</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wash off chalky oxidised surface</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sheet peeling with powder on the back</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Let it dry fully after washing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Adhesion failure across the whole area</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fill cracks and let filler cure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Soft, wrinkled patches over repairs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Seal on condition, not annually</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brittle buildup and crazed flaking</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two thin coats, not one thick</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Trapped solvent and weak layers</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Check the overnight low and dew point</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dew damage on an uncured coat</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Keep traffic off for the full cure</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Wheel track peeling</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The pattern is consistent: nearly every entry in that table is something done before or immediately after the sealer goes down, and none of it is about which product was in the pail. A mid-range sealer on a properly prepared driveway outlasts a premium one applied over dust every time.
          </p>

          <p>
            For the full preparation sequence, see our guides to <a href="/how-to-clean-asphalt-driveway-before-sealing/">cleaning an asphalt driveway before sealing</a> and <a href="/how-to-seal-a-driveway/">how to seal a driveway</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="concrete-vs-asphalt" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Peeling on Concrete Is a Different Problem
          </h2>

          <p>
            Everything above assumes asphalt. Concrete sealers fail by peeling too, and the causes only partly overlap, so applying asphalt logic to a concrete drive leads people to the wrong fix.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cause</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Asphalt</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Concrete</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Surface contamination</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Common</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Common</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Coating buildup</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very common</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Common with film-formers</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Moisture from beneath the slab</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rare</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very common</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Applied over a previous sealer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Common</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very common</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Efflorescence under the film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not applicable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Common</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wrong sealer chemistry for the slab</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rare</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Common</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The moisture row is the important difference. Concrete on grade wicks vapour up from the ground continuously, and a film-forming sealer traps it until it lifts, exactly as happens with garage floor coatings. Asphalt is flexible and porous enough that this is rarely the cause there, so a concrete drive that peels warrants a moisture test that an asphalt one does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other concrete-specific answer is chemistry. Penetrating sealers soak into the slab rather than forming a film, so there is nothing to peel and vapour passes through freely. Where a film-former has failed twice on the same concrete, switching to a penetrating product usually ends the problem rather than repeating it. See our guides to <a href="/best-concrete-sealer/">concrete sealers</a> and <a href="/how-to-seal-a-concrete-driveway/">sealing a concrete driveway</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Recoating Over the Peeling</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A new coat bonds to the failing layer beneath it, not to the driveway, so it fails with it and usually faster. Everything loose has to come off first, and the removal needs to extend past the visibly peeling area, because the edge of an adhesion failure is rarely where it stops.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming It Was a Bad Product</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Peeling is an adhesion failure, and adhesion is decided by what was on the driveway when the sealer went down. Oil, dust, chalky oxidation and moisture cause far more failures than product quality does. Look at the underside of a flake before blaming the pail.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Every Year Because It Seems Diligent</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer accumulates, and a thick built-up coating goes rigid, loses the flexibility to move with the asphalt, and crazes and flakes off in sheets. Flakes with old sealer rather than asphalt on the back are the signature. Seal on condition rather than on the calendar.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying One Thick Coat to Save Time</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heavy coat skins over on top while the layer beneath is still full of solvent and moisture, which produces a weak film that lifts later. Two thin coats cure properly and bond better, and the difference shows up in the second season rather than immediately.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Checking Only the Daytime Forecast</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A warm dry afternoon means nothing if a cold clear night deposits dew on a coating that has not finished curing, which acts like light rain. Check the overnight low and the dew point as well as the daytime conditions, particularly when sealing in autumn.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my driveway sealer peeling off?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Because it never bonded. Something sat between the sealer and the asphalt, most often oil, dust or chalky oxidised surface, or the driveway was damp when it went down. Look at the underside of a flake: clean and glossy means an adhesion failure rather than a product fault.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal over peeling sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The new coat bonds to the failing layer rather than the driveway, so it lifts along with it, usually sooner. Everything loose has to be scraped, brushed and washed off first, and the removal should extend past the visibly peeling area.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you seal a driveway too many times?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is a common cause of flaking. Sealer accumulates, and thick buildup goes rigid, loses the flexibility to move with the asphalt, then crazes and sheets off. Flakes with old sealer rather than asphalt attached to the back are the tell.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my sealer peeling only in the wheel tracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Traffic before the coating fully cured. Sealer is dry to the touch long before it has hardened, and turning tyres apply a shearing force that lifts it. The rest of the driveway being intact is the diagnostic, and the fix is keeping traffic off for the full cure time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if it is the sealer or the driveway failing?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look at what is stuck to the underside of a flake. Clean and glossy means the sealer let go of the asphalt, which is an adhesion problem you can correct. Asphalt aggregate attached means the surface itself is coming apart, which is a resurfacing question instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does oil cause driveway sealer to peel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, reliably, and at exactly the spots where it was. Oil soaks into porous asphalt and washing does not lift what has penetrated, so it needs a degreaser or an absorbent poultice. Every oil spot left untreated becomes a place the sealer lifts first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will pressure washing remove peeling sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It removes what is already loose, which is a necessary first step, but it will not strip a fully bonded old coating. Where buildup is the cause and the whole layer has to come off, chemical stripping or mechanical removal is usually the realistic route.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should I wait before driving on new sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Longer than it takes to feel dry, and the product sheet is the authority. Dry, cured and ready for traffic are three different states, and driving too early is one of the most common causes of peeling confined to the wheel tracks.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Related Guides -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Related Guides</h2>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
            <a href="/driveway-sealer-still-sticky/" class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 hover:shadow-md transition-shadow no-underline group">
              <p class="text-[#FF4500] font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Troubleshooting</p>
              <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-bold text-lg group-hover:text-[#1E90FF] transition-colors">Sealer Still Sticky?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mt-2">The other common failure, and why it is a different cause.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/how-to-remove-old-driveway-sealer/" class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 hover:shadow-md transition-shadow no-underline group">
              <p class="text-[#1E90FF] font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Remediation</p>
              <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-bold text-lg group-hover:text-[#1E90FF] transition-colors">Removing Old Driveway Sealer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mt-2">What it takes to strip a failing built-up coating.</p>
            </a>
            <a href="/how-often-should-you-seal-your-driveway/" class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 hover:shadow-md transition-shadow no-underline group">
              <p class="text-[#16A34A] font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Frequency</p>
              <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-bold text-lg group-hover:text-[#1E90FF] transition-colors">How Often to Seal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mt-2">Why sealing every year causes the flaking it was meant to prevent.</p>
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Peeling means the sealer never bonded, so the cause is almost always in the preparation rather than the product. Start by looking at the underside of a flake: clean and glossy points to contamination or damp, powder points to chalky oxidation, old sealer points to buildup, and asphalt aggregate means the driveway itself is failing.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The six causes are contamination, too many accumulated coats, moisture and weather, traffic before cure, sealing over uncured crack filler, and incompatible products. Oil is the most stubborn, because it penetrates and washing will not lift it. Buildup is the most ironic, since it is caused by sealing too diligently.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Whatever the cause, it has to come off before anything goes back on, and the removal needs to extend past the visible failure. Then fix the underlying cause, let the surface dry longer than it looks like it needs, and apply two thin coats rather than one thick one, in weather that includes a check of the overnight low.
          </p>
        </div>


